head 274ed845), so the merge moved the provenance and not the bytes.

zk-cred-longfellow ships this file too, and the two must agree. They are NOT
byte-identical today: Longfellow v0.1.2's copy is sha256 697d016b..., the
pre-redirect version, because the derive_redirect and rejects_redirect arrays
were added in vega and Longfellow has not mirrored them yet.

The arrays they BOTH carry - version, min_nonce_chars, derive, digest,
rejects - must be identical, and TestVendoredVectorsAgreeWithLongfellow (a
zknative test, since Longfellow's copy is only staged in that build) enforces
exactly that. It is the check that would catch the library changing a
transcript shape under this file. Tighten it to full equality once Longfellow
mirrors the redirect arrays.

These vectors are what tells this verifier and the two native provers that
they agree about what a presentation is bound to. Vendored rather than
fetched so the test suite does not depend on the network, and the digest is
pinned in challenge_vectors_test.go.

Re-vendor rather than edit. A local change turns a cross-implementation check
into a self-consistency one that passes while the verifier and the provers
disagree - which is the single failure this file exists to prevent.
